Programming Assignment 2: Distributed Bellman-Ford
==================================================

Team
----
  Noa Keter, ID 318875770
  Ido Josephsbeg, ID 322641135

Overview
--------
bfproc is the node process for the distributed Bellman-Ford algorithm. Every node
communicates only with its direct neighbours, through the provided netproc
simulator, and from those exchanges it works out three things: the root, which is
the smallest node ID in the reachable network, the cost of its shortest path to
that root, and its parent, meaning the next-hop node on the way to the root. We
wrote only the node process. netproc is given and we do not modify it.


Building
--------
Run "make" to produce the executable named bfproc, and "make clean" to remove the
build products. The code is compiled with:

  -Wall -Wextra -O2 -std=c11 -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L

The _POSIX_C_SOURCE definition is necessary because we use clock_gettime and
CLOCK_REALTIME, which are POSIX rather than ISO C. Under strict -std=c11 they are
not declared without it, so the macro is part of CFLAGS in the Makefile rather
than something the grader needs to supply. The build is clean, with no warnings.


Running
-------
  bfproc <netproc_address> <node_id> <lifetime_sec> <cost1> [cost2 ...]

For example:

  ./bfproc 127.0.0.1 2 16 $(./costs.sh topology.csv 2)

netproc_address is the IP of the host running netproc. The port is fixed at 6789.
node_id is this node's integer ID, and lifetime_sec is how many seconds the
process runs before shutting down gracefully. The remaining arguments are the
link costs in port order, exactly as the provided costs.sh script emits them
(that is, in the order the links appear in the configuration file). Cost number i
on the command line corresponds to the local link index i that netproc uses to
tag the frames arriving on that link.


Source files
------------
  bf.h         Provided constants (PORT, HELLO_TIMEOUT, ROOT_TIMEOUT), unmodified.
  proto.h      The wire framing and the update-message struct, with pack/unpack.
  time_util.h  now_ms(), the single CLOCK_REALTIME time source (header-only).
  net.h, net.c     Socket setup, the ID handshake, and 128-byte frame I/O.
  bfnode.h, bfnode.c  The node state and the Bellman-Ford update, refresh, and
                      expiry logic, kept free of any socket code.
  bfproc.c     main(): argument parsing, the select() event loop, the timers, and
               all of the printed output.


Design
------
Because a node has only one socket to watch (its connection to netproc), we chose
a single-threaded design built around select(), rather than a multi-threaded one
with a timer per thread. This avoids any need for locking and keeps the output
ordering deterministic, which matters because a state line must be printed only
when the root or the cost changes, while a "message sent" line is printed on
every send.

We keep three timers, each stored as an absolute deadline in milliseconds: the
hello timer, which guarantees a message at least every HELLO_TIMEOUT seconds and
is reset whenever we send, the root-expiry timer, which fires when the current
(non-self) root information expires, and the lifetime timer. On each pass through
the loop we take the soonest of the three deadlines, subtract the current time,
express the result as a struct timeval, and hand that to select() as its timeout.
This is the approach the course FAQ recommends, namely doing the arithmetic on a
struct timeval. When select() returns, we check the conditions in a fixed order:
lifetime first, then any incoming frame, then root expiry, then the hello timer.


Data structures
---------------
The whole of a node's view lives in one struct, node_state (see bfnode.h): my_id,
my_root and my_cost, parent_link (the local link index of the next hop) together
with parent_id (the next hop's node ID, which is what we print), exp_deadline_ms,
the absolute time at which the current root information expires (infinite while we
are our own root), and costs[], the per-link cost array with its length.


Packet structure
----------------
netproc always transfers exactly 128 bytes at a time: one link byte followed by a
127-byte payload. On the way out, byte zero is the link number, and we always use
0xFF to broadcast. netproc rewrites that byte to the receiver's own link index
and copies the 127 payload bytes through unchanged, so from a node's point of view
both directions are simply 128-byte frames.

The 127-byte payload carries the update message, defined as struct bf_msg in
proto.h. It uses the first 16 bytes and zero-pads the rest. All four fields are
32-bit and sent in network byte order: root (the smallest known ID), cost (the
cost to that root), id (the sender, which becomes the receiver's parent), and
exp_ms (the remaining lifetime, in milliseconds). The payload is private to
bfproc, since netproc never looks inside it and the grader runs only our own
bfproc instances together, so we were free to choose the layout and units.


Multiple timeouts and the choice of units
-----------------------------------------
Every internal duration in the program, both the timer deadlines and the expTime
field, is expressed in milliseconds. The constants in bf.h, HELLO_TIMEOUT (2) and
ROOT_TIMEOUT (6), are in seconds, so they are multiplied by 1000 at each point
where they are used. We treated that conversion as an explicit invariant and
noted it in a comment at every such site, since a forgotten factor of 1000 is the
one place a unit bug could hide.

We chose milliseconds for expTime deliberately. The assignment gives no unit for
it on the wire (the "expTime = 100" in the text is only an illustration), and the
rules that do matter are unit-agnostic: a message originates with expTime equal to
ROOT_TIMEOUT, real elapsed time is subtracted on every forward, and the value may
only increase when a fresher message arrives for the same root. Since output is
printed to a tenth of a second and the timeouts are 2 and 6 seconds, aging needs
sub-second resolution. An integer-second expTime would truncate and drift, which
could cause premature or late expiry. Milliseconds avoid that.

Aging is implemented with absolute deadlines. When a node adopts root information
it records exp_deadline = now + exp_ms, and when it forwards it sends
exp_ms = deadline - now. The example from the assignment, where a message received
at time 3 with expTime 100 is resent at time 12 with expTime 91, then falls out
naturally. A message about the same root refreshes the deadline only when it would
push it later, which is how we honour the rule that expTime may only increase. A
node that is its own root has an infinite deadline and originates exp_ms equal to
ROOT_TIMEOUT in milliseconds. When a deadline passes, the node resets to being its
own root, which is how the algorithm recovers from a root crash.


The update logic
----------------
When a message with root R, cost C, sender S and expTime E arrives on link L whose
cost is lc, the candidate cost to R through that neighbour is C + lc. If R is
smaller than our current root we always adopt it, taking S as our parent and
C + lc as our cost, and we start a fresh deadline. If R equals our current root
and we are not our own root, we first refresh the deadline if E would make it
later. We then follow our current parent if the message came from it, accepting
its cost even if it has increased, which is what keeps distance-vector correct in
the presence of loops. Otherwise we switch to S only if it offers a strictly
cheaper path, and on an equal-cost tie we keep our current parent so the tree does
not flap. If R is larger than our root we ignore the message. We print a new state
line and broadcast only when the root or the cost actually changed. Refreshing a
deadline on its own is not a change.


Output format
-------------
Times are printed relative to when the process started. We capture start_ms once
at the top of main() and, for each event, print (now_ms() - start_ms) / 1000 using
the required format. This still satisfies Note 1 in the assignment, since the
clock source is CLOCK_REALTIME and the relative time is simply one CLOCK_REALTIME
reading minus another, and it matches the small, monotonically increasing
timestamps in the example. The initial state is therefore printed at about
time 0.0. The three formats are:

  time=%.01f\tRoot=%d\tparent=%d\tdistance=%d\n
  time=%.01f\tMessage sent to all neighbors\n
  time=%.01f\tLifetime expired. Shutting down.\n

When a node is its own root it prints parent=NULL and distance 0. Otherwise the
parent is printed as the parent node's ID, taken from the chosen message's id
field. We read this from the assignment's own example, where a node with four
links prints parent=217: that cannot be a link index, which would be 0 to 3, so
the value being asked for must be a node ID.


Socket details
--------------
Following the course FAQ, we set TCP_NODELAY on the socket, receive with
MSG_WAITALL so that a whole 128-byte frame arrives in a single call (a readn loop
is kept as a defensive fallback for a short read), and send with MSG_NOSIGNAL so
that a dead netproc produces an EPIPE error instead of a SIGPIPE that would kill
the process. An EPIPE on send, or a recv that returns zero, is treated as netproc
having gone away, and the node then cleans up and exits. The handshake is simple:
right after connect() the node sends its ID as a four-byte integer in network byte
order. When the lifetime expires the node prints the shutdown line, closes the
socket, frees its memory, and exits.


Testing
-------
We tested against the provided netproc. The two scenarios below use a line
topology of three nodes, which is described by this configuration file (the same
format netproc and costs.sh expect, one "endpoint1,endpoint2,cost" per line):

  1,2,10
  2,3,5

With every node started at once, the network converges to root 1: node 2 reaches
it directly with parent 1 and distance 10, and node 3 reaches it through node 2
with parent 2 and distance 15. Hello beacons appear every two seconds and each
node shuts down cleanly when its lifetime ends.

To check root-crash recovery we gave node 1 a short lifetime so that it leaves
after about three seconds. Roughly ROOT_TIMEOUT seconds later, nodes 2 and 3 let
their root-1 information expire and each becomes its own root, and shortly
afterwards the network re-elects node 2 as the new root, with node 3 settling on
parent 2 and distance 5.
